this morning the Supreme Court hears a  case pitting Free Speech against gay  rights a Christian website designer is  challenging a Colorado law that requires  her to create wedding websites for  same-sex couples even though she opposes  same-sex marriage Jan Crawford is  outside the Supreme Court Jan good  morning so what would we hear today  well good morning Nate you know this  case really raises interesting questions  for artists and designers who might be  asked to create messages they disagree  with so it's kind of pitting the First  Amendment against anti-discrimination  law now Colorado website designer Lori  Smith says she'll help any client with  their work or their branding but she  won't design a website that will  contradict her Christian religious  beliefs and that includes creating  websites for same-sex Weddings But that  refusal would violate Colorado's  anti-discrimination law which would  require her to design websites for all  couples she says the government can't  make her create messages that that would  violate Her First Amendment rights now  State Attorney General Phil Weiser says  the law is about making sure lgbtq  people are treated equally so that you  know businesses can't just turn them  away now the court had a similar case  five years ago involving a baker he won  that case but narrowly the justices  didn't take up these bigger First  Amendment issues so now it's back before  this newly conservative of Supreme Court  Gail  Jan thank you very much
 

